# d27_named_groups / case_sensitivity.rxt
#
# PROMISE under test: a group NAME is case-sensitive identity, distinct
# from CASELESS matching of the group's CONTENT. "name" and "NAME" are
# two different names (two distinct groups, both capturing
# independently), and that holds whether or not the block matches
# caselessly -- caseless affects which bytes the group's own pattern
# text matches, never whether two names are "the same name".
#
# Oracle: python3 `re`, on the (?P<name>...) translation, verified via
# m.span(k) and c.groupindex (both names present as distinct keys) --
# see oracle.py. Both blocks below are ordinary python-verifiable
# match/capture cases, no pcre2-only marker needed: python and PCRE2
# agree here (measured, docs/testing.md's caseless mapping -- `flags i`
# -> `re.IGNORECASE | re.ASCII` -- carries the same distinction).
#
# Scope note: the second block below uses this format's block-scoped
# `flags i` directive (pcrec -i, PCRE2_CASELESS at the whole-pattern
# level), not the inline `(?i)` modifier construct -- inline modifiers
# are module `modifiers`, a different promise, and mixing it in here
# would smuggle a second module's axis into a named-groups block
# (docs/testing.md's differential-gate principle: enable exactly the
# module the block is testing).

# two case-differing names, no caseless matching: two distinct groups,
# each capturing its own span
pattern (?<name>a)(?<NAME>b)
features named-groups
m "xabx" 1 3
g 1 1 2
g 2 2 3

# same shape under `flags i`: the pair still compiles (case-differing
# names are still two names, not a duplicate), captures stay distinct
# by POSITION exactly as above, and caseless matching is visible only
# in which bytes matched (upper-case "AB" here, where the pattern text
# itself says lower-case a/b)
pattern (?<name>a)(?<NAME>b)
features named-groups
flags i
m "xABx" 1 3
g 1 1 2
g 2 2 3
